From: SPIranha <https://github.com/MTSistemi/SPIranha>
Subject: [PATCH] never let an SPI operation hang the board

An S_CMD_O_SPIOP while the SPI peripheral is disabled never returns:
spi_write_blocking waits on a FIFO that cannot drain, the main loop stops, and
with it tud_task(). USB stops being serviced, so the board vanishes from the
host entirely - the port cannot even be opened again - until someone unplugs
it. No amount of care on the host side can recover it.

The host is supposed to send S_CMD_S_PIN_STATE first, and flashrom does. But
flashrom also sends S_CMD_S_PIN_STATE(0) when it exits, so the board is left
with SPI disabled: the *next* program to talk to it hangs it by asking a
perfectly ordinary question. That is a loaded gun pointed at every host that
was not written by us.

Found the hard way, on a board that then had to be unplugged.

Enabling on demand is what the operation needs anyway, costs one branch, and
makes the failure impossible rather than merely unlikely.
---
diff --git a/main.c b/main.c
--- a/main.c
+++ b/main.c
@@ -181,6 +181,15 @@ void s_cmd_o_spiop() {
 	readbytes_blocking(&wlen, 3);
 	readbytes_blocking(&rlen, 3);

+	/* An SPI operation while the peripheral is disabled never returns: the
+	 * write blocks on a FIFO that will not drain, the main loop stops, and
+	 * with it the USB task - the board disappears until it is unplugged.
+	 * The host is supposed to send S_CMD_S_PIN_STATE first, and flashrom
+	 * leaves the pins disabled when it exits, so the very next host to talk
+	 * to the board can hang it by asking politely. Enable instead. */
+	if (!spi_enabled)
+		enable_spi();
+
 	cs_select(cs_pin);

 	while (wlen) {
